      "text": "Method 1: Repeated subbing in and combining like terms.\n\\begin{align*}\n[f(x) + 2f(1 - x)] + 2[f(1 - x) + 2f(x)] &= 3x^2 + 3(1 - x)^2 \\\\\n&= 6x^2 - 6x + 3.\n\\end{align*}Substituting $x = 3,$ we get \\[3f(3) + 2f(2) + 2f(3) = 27 - 18 + 3,\\]or $5f(3) = 12,$ so \\[f(3) = \\boxed{\\frac{12}{5}}.\\]*Solution 2: Next, we define a function $g(x),$ such that \\[g(x) + g(2 - x) = f(x) + f(1 - x).\\]Then letting $x = 1 - x,$ we get \\[f(x) + f(2 - x) = g(2 - x) + g(x).\\]Subtracting these two equations, we get \\[f(x) - f(2 - x) = g(x) - g(2 - x),\\]so the function is odd. Thus, \\[f(2) + f(0) = 0,\\]so \\[f(2) = -f(0).\\]Thus, \\[g(2) + f(1) = 3,\\]and $g(2)$ is the point where the function $g$ has an $x$ value equal to $2.$ Finally, let $g(2) = -m,$ so \\[f(2) + f(0) = g(2) + f(2) = m + f(2) = 0.$ Then \\[\\boxed{f(3) = \\frac{12}{5}}.\\]",
