      "text": "To solve for \\( f(3) \\), we will first express \\( f(x) \\) in terms of \\( x \\) using the given functional equation. Let \\( g(x) = f(x) + 2f(1-x) \\). We know that \\( g(x) = 3x^2 \\).\n\nFirst, we need to express \\( f(x) \\) directly from the functional equation. From the definition of \\( g(x) \\), we have:\n\\[ g(x) = f(x) + 2f(1-x) = 3x^2. \\]\nWe also need the expression for \\( f(1-x) \\):\n\\[ g(1-x) = f(1-x) + 2f(x) = 3(1-x)^2 = 3(1 - 2x + x^2) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2. \\]\nNow we have two equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf(x) + 2f(1-x) = 3x^2 \\\\\nf(1-x) + 2f(x) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nLet's subtract the second equation from the first:\n\\[ \\left( f(x) + 2f(1-x) \\right) - (f(1-x) + 2f(x)) = 3x^2 - (3 - 6x + 3x^2), \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ f(1-x) - f(1-x) = 3x^2 - 3 + 6x - 3x^2, \\]\nand further simplifies to:\n\\[ 0 = 3x^2 - 6x - 3, \\]\nor\n\\[ f(1-x) = f(x) - (2x + 1). \\]\nSince \\( g(x) = f(x) + 2f(1-x) \\), we can substitute \\( f(1-x) = f(x) - (2x + 1) \\) into \\( g(x) \\):\n\\[ f(x) + 2(f(x) - (2x + 1)) = 3x^2, \\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(x) - 4x - 2 = 3x^2, \\]\nor\n\\[ 3f(x) - 4x - 2 = 3x^2, \\]\nwhich gives\n\\[ f(x) = x^2 + \\frac{4x}{3} + \\frac{2}{3}. \\]\nNow we can find \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[ f(3) = 3^2 + \\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = 9 + 4 + \\frac{2}{3} = 13 + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{41}{3}. \\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{41}{3}}.\nLet's verify this by coding the solution in Python.\n```python\n# Define the function f(x) based on the derived expression\ndef f(x):\n    return x**2 + (4*x)/3 + (2)/3\n\n# Calculate f(3)\nf_3 = f(3)\nprint(f_3)\n```",
